''' __init__.py is a special Python file that is used to indicate that the directory it is present in is a Python package. 
It can be an empty file or can contain initialization code for the package. When a package is imported, the __init__.py file is executed,
allowing you to define package-level variables, functions, or classes.
In Python 3.3 and later, the __init__.py file is not strictly required for a directory to be recognized as a package, 
but it is still commonly used for backward compatibility and to provide package-level functionality.'''